intent: >
  Make the site obviously navigable for older / less tech-savvy visitors
  by bumping base font size, promoting the phone CTA, adding scroll
  affordance to the homepage hero, and making service-card clickability
  visually explicit.

slice: "?/?"
kind: feature
behavior_change: additive

reversibility: trivial

scope:
  declared:
    - src/assets/css/main.css
    - src/_includes/partials/nav.njk
    - src/index.njk
  actual:
    - src/assets/css/main.css
    - src/_includes/partials/nav.njk
    - src/index.njk
  drift: []

proof:
  tests_run: "Manual: dev server + Chrome via mcp__claude-in-chrome. Verified html font-size: 18px, nav links 20.25px, phone CTA 59px tall with icon, hero is 790px vs 929px viewport (85vh), About section peeks above fold on both desktop and mobile, scroll indicator present, 7 'Learn more' affordances on the service grid cards, mobile menu button 54x68 (over 44pt minimum), no horizontal overflow at mobile viewport."
  result: green
  failing: 0

tests:
  needed: "no"
  rationale: >
    Visual UX changes; no logic. Verified by computed-style inspection
    and bounding-rect measurements across desktop and mobile viewports.

assumptions:
  - "Bumping base font to 18px proportionally affects all Tailwind text-* classes (text-base -> 18px, text-lg -> 20.25px, etc.). Verified by sampling computed sizes in the browser."
  - "Tailwind's animate-bounce is acceptable for the scroll indicator. Some accessibility guidelines flag animations for vestibular sensitivity, but a single subtle bounce on a single element falls under most prefers-reduced-motion exceptions. Could be wrapped in a media query if anyone reports motion sickness."
  - "Older visitors browse with default (not zoomed) fonts. The 18px bump assumes they haven't already set a 125% browser zoom; if they have, this stacks with that and may be too large. Acceptable trade-off; visitors who already zoom are aware they're doing it."

specifications:
  - "html { font-size: 18px } is the body-plan move. Every text size on the site downstream of it scales proportionally."
  - "Focus-visible uses lime (the site CTA color) for visual consistency: hover and keyboard focus produce the same color cue."
  - "Hero is min-h-[85vh] not min-h-screen. The 15vh slice that's not covered shows the About section above the fold."
  - "Scroll indicator is a link to #about, not just visual. Clicking/tapping it scrolls down — useful for older users who recognize it as 'click here for more'."
  - "Service cards display 'Learn more ->' explicitly. The card-as-link pattern is preserved; the affordance is added."

deviations_from_plan: >
  None. Approved scope was 'quick wins + scroll affordance + link affordance'.
  All three landed.

trade_offs: >
  Chose to NOT do a full Lighthouse audit pass. User chose the quick-wins
  option explicitly. Most-likely Lighthouse gaps (contrast on specific
  text, missing alt text, ARIA labels) can be addressed in a follow-up if
  Steve sees specific issues.

  Chose to NOT add a sticky 'Call Steve' banner across every page. Same
  rationale: user chose quick wins, not the more aggressive option.

  Chose 18px base, not 19 or 20. 18 is the standard 'comfortable reading'
  base for older audiences without making the layout feel cramped. The
  whole site rescales from this number; pushing higher would have started
  to break visual rhythm in compact components (nav, footer).

  Chose 85vh for the hero, not 70 or 75. 85 keeps the hero feeling like a
  proper hero (still feels like the main impression on load) while showing
  enough of the next section to signal scroll. 70vh would have felt small.

  Em-dash inherited from a previously merged PR (lines 650, 662 of
  index.njk) NOT cleaned up here. Out of scope and would muddy the diff.
  Separate housekeeping PR if Steve cares about typography that fine.

interfaces: {}

self_review:
  consistency: >
    Nav style change uses the same Tailwind vocabulary (text-lg,
    font-semibold, rounded-lg, shadow-md) the rest of the site uses for
    CTAs. Phone icon uses Font Awesome (already on the site). Service
    card 'Learn more' uses the same forest -> lime hover transition as
    every other internal link on the site.
  metz: N/A — templates and CSS.
  body_plan: >
    Three distinct changes, three distinct objects:
      - main.css owns site-wide visual defaults (font size, focus)
      - nav.njk owns the header / phone CTA
      - index.njk hero owns the homepage scroll-affordance moment
      - index.njk service grid owns the homepage link-affordance moment
    Each object has a clear refusal list. main.css refuses to know about
    page-specific layouts. nav.njk refuses to know about page content.
    Hero refuses to load services or projects. Service cards refuse to
    do anything except link to their pages.
  honesty: >
    The scroll affordance is real (hero is genuinely less than viewport
    height now). The Learn more text is real (the cards always linked
    somewhere; this just labels it). The phone CTA promotion is real
    (it's now bigger and harder to miss). No theater.

confidence: high
reviewer_attention:
  - "src/assets/css/main.css:50-58  — the 18px base. Single most impactful change in the PR. Worth reading the comment to understand why it cascades."
  - "src/index.njk:7-21  — hero height + scroll indicator. The page-flow change."
  - "src/_includes/partials/nav.njk:17-20  — phone CTA promotion. Verify it doesn't crowd the rest of the nav on tablet widths."
